# Production incident (2026-09-18): the two [[redirects]] rules added to netlify.toml for the
# same-origin auth proxy (see its own history/comments, and auth-api-base-url.ts) never took
# effect — confirmed live (POST /api/auth/login and /api/auth/refresh both 404'd on every
# browser, breaking login entirely). Root cause: Netlify processes this _redirects file BEFORE
# netlify.toml, and the catch-all rule below matched every path first, including /api/auth/*.
# For GET this returned this app's own index.html (200 — indistinguishable from "working" at a
# glance); for POST, Netlify does not serve a static-file rewrite target, so it fell through to
# a genuine 404 instead. Moving the two specific rules here, ahead of the catch-all, is what
# actually works — this exact file's own priority over netlify.toml is what proved the bug in
# the first place, so it's also the mechanism used to fix it. See netlify.toml for why only the
# production backend is targeted (staging is intentionally excluded for now).
/api/auth/*               https://wohnungen-api.onrender.com/api/auth/:splat               200!
/api/security/csrf-token  https://wohnungen-api.onrender.com/api/security/csrf-token       200!

# Original rule — SPA fallback. Only a safety net: this app uses Angular's hash-based routing
# (withHashLocation() in app.config.ts), so every real in-app route is a #/... fragment the
# server never sees; this exists only for a stray non-hash URL (an old bookmark, a search
# engine indexing something unexpected) to still get the app shell instead of a raw 404.
/*    /index.html   200
